<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Additional Results for Bellman-Ford</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix contains additional results from experiments in <a href="#S5" title="5 Case Study: Bellman-Ford ‣ MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> (<a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Circuit Sparsification ‣ Appendix A Additional Results for Bellman-Ford ‣ MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>), as well as relevant plots from runs with different training data (<a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Bellman-Ford without Self-Loops ‣ Appendix A Additional Results for Bellman-Ford ‣ MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>) and seeds (<a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Results from different runs ‣ Appendix A Additional Results for Bellman-Ford ‣ MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Score Comparison for Bellman-Ford</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare in <a href="#A1.F7" title="In A.1 Score Comparison for Bellman-Ford ‣ Appendix A Additional Results for Bellman-Ford ‣ MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> different scoring methods for the Bellman-Ford GNN in <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 The Bellman-Ford Circuit ‣ 5 Case Study: Bellman-Ford ‣ MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>. Only <span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">WeightGrad</span> fails to find the optimal 10-edge circuit, instead requiring 12 edges (<a href="#A1.F8" title="In A.1 Score Comparison for Bellman-Ford ‣ Appendix A Additional Results for Bellman-Ford ‣ MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21442v1/circuits_bellman_ford_edge_count_seed_0.svg" id="A1.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/126;" width="476" height="126"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="A1.F7.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Circuit loss (left) and ablated circuit loss (right) for circuits found using Weight, <span id="A1.F7.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">WeightGrad</span>, EAP, and EAP-IG (<math id="A1.F7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=20</annotation></semantics></math>) with <math id="A1.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=1,2,\dots,10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=1,2,\dots,10</annotation></semantics></math> for the Bellman-Ford network.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21442v1/bellman_ford_weight_grad_circuit_seed_0.svg" id="A1.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:167/302;" width="167" height="302"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="A1.F8.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Best Bellman-Ford circuit found by <span id="A1.F8.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">WeightGrad</span>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Circuit Sparsification</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we demonstrate how the circuit in <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 The Bellman-Ford Circuit ‣ 5 Case Study: Bellman-Ford ‣ MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> (<a href="#S5.F2" title="In 5.2 The Bellman-Ford Circuit ‣ 5 Case Study: Bellman-Ford ‣ MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) emerges during training by extracting circuits from earlier epochs. We identify sufficient circuits at epochs 1000 and 2000. The final circuit appears by epoch 3000.
This mirrors observations in LLMs where features may not align with individual neurons, but limiting capacity (either architecturally or in our case through regularization) discourages instances where a single feature is represented by multiple neurons <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Elhage et al., 2022</a>]</cite>. (In contrast, expanding model capacity has been observed in to encourage so-called <em id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">polysemantic</em> neurons to split into several <em id="A1.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">monosemantic</em> neurons in LLMs.)</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21442v1/bellman_ford_circuit_epoch_1000_seed_0.svg" id="A1.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/165;" width="476" height="165"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="A1.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Bellman-Ford circuit at epoch 1000. The circuit contains 73 edges and achieves <math id="A1.F9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{Mult}}=0.0578" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Mult</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0578</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{Mult}}=0.0578</annotation></semantics></math>. Elements which are part of the final circuit are bolded.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21442v1/bellman_ford_circuit_epoch_2000_seed_0.svg" id="A1.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/254;" width="405" height="254"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F10.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>: </span><span id="A1.F10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Bellman-Ford circuit at epoch 2000. The circuit 31 edges and achieves <math id="A1.F10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{Mult}}=0.0565" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Mult</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0565</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{Mult}}=0.0565</annotation></semantics></math>. Elements which are part of the final circuit are bolded.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Bellman-Ford without Self-Loops</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A previous version of this work contained experiments from <a href="#S5" title="5 Case Study: Bellman-Ford ‣ MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> in which the training and testing graphs did not have self-loops. As a result, while the minimum aggregation in <a href="#S5.E9" title="In 5.1 Training ‣ 5 Case Study: Bellman-Ford ‣ MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> returned the optimal neighbor embedding for each node, it did not include the node’s own embedding. Hence the update MLP <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{Up}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>Up</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{Up}}</annotation></semantics></math> became responsible for comparing between a node’s current shortest path distance and that of its best neighbor. While self-loops have been added in <a href="#S5" title="5 Case Study: Bellman-Ford ‣ MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we briefly study the behavior of the model trained without self-loops to highlight how MINAR reveals the emergence of this ability to compute the minimum between two elements.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The MinAggGNN is trained on the same data as <a href="#S5" title="5 Case Study: Bellman-Ford ‣ MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, achieving an MSE Loss of <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MSE}}=0.0002" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MSE</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0002</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{MSE}}=0.0002</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{Mult}}=0.0642" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Mult</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0642</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{Mult}}=0.0642</annotation></semantics></math>. The circuit (<a href="#A1.F12" title="In A.3 Bellman-Ford without Self-Loops ‣ Appendix A Additional Results for Bellman-Ford ‣ MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), consisting of 17 edges and discovered using EAP-IG (<math id="A1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=20</annotation></semantics></math>) achieves a test loss of <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{Mult}}=0.0599" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Mult</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0599</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{Mult}}=0.0599</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F11" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21442v1/bellman_ford_no_self_loops_loss.svg" id="A1.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/113;" width="238" height="113"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F11.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 11</span>: </span><span id="A1.F11.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Train loss, test loss, and <math id="A1.F11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> regularization term for Bellman-Ford MinAggGNN trained without self-loops.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F12" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21442v1/images/bellman-ford-no-self-loop/no_self_loops_circuit_with_colorbar.png" id="A1.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:238/276;" width="238" height="276" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F12.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 12</span>: </span><span id="A1.F12.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Bellman-Ford circuit from MinAggGNN trained without self-loops. Contains 17 of 18,240 edges. <math id="A1.F12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{Mult}}=0.0599" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Mult</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0599</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{Mult}}=0.0599</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we wish to highlight the nodes for the first update MLP (beginning with <span id="A1.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">convs.0.up_mlp</span>). Manually inspecting the weights, we see that this portion of the circuit implements the minimum operation between the initial node embedding <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{self}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>self</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{self}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the best possible update among its neighbors in the first step, which we will denote <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>nbr</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="A1.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{convs.0.up\_mlp.lins.1.6}=\begin{bmatrix}0&amp;\phantom{-}0.9620\end{bmatrix}\ReLU\left(\begin{bmatrix}0.4921&amp;0\\
0.5905&amp;-0.6102\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}\\
x_{\text{self}}\end{bmatrix}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">convs.0.up_mlp.lins.1.6</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0.9620</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ReLU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0.4921</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0.5905</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.6102</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>nbr</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>self</mtext></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{convs.0.up\_mlp.lins.1.6}=\begin{bmatrix}0&amp;\phantom{-}0.9620\end{bmatrix}\ReLU\left(\begin{bmatrix}0.4921&amp;0\\
0.5905&amp;-0.6102\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}\\
x_{\text{self}}\end{bmatrix}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5905x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}-0.6102x_{\text{self}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0.5905</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>nbr</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>0.6102</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>self</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5905x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}-0.6102x_{\text{self}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. if roughly <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}&gt;x_{\text{self}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>nbr</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>self</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}&gt;x_{\text{self}}</annotation></semantics></math>), then this becomes</p>
<table id="A1.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{bmatrix}0.5681&amp;-0.5870\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}\\
x_{\text{self}}\end{bmatrix}\propto x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}-x_{\text{self}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mn>0.5681</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5870</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>nbr</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>self</mtext></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>nbr</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>self</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{bmatrix}0.5681&amp;-0.5870\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}\\
x_{\text{self}}\end{bmatrix}\propto x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}-x_{\text{self}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Otherwise, if <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5905x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}-0.6102x_{\text{self}}&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0.5905</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>nbr</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>0.6102</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>self</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5905x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}-0.6102x_{\text{self}}&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. if roughly <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}&lt;x_{\text{self}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>nbr</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>self</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}&lt;x_{\text{self}}</annotation></semantics></math>), then this becomes 0.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Notice in <a href="#A1.F12" title="In A.3 Bellman-Ford without Self-Loops ‣ Appendix A Additional Results for Bellman-Ford ‣ MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> the primary input to the second step of Bellman-Ford comes from <span id="A1.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">convs.0.up_mlp.lins.1.2</span>. If <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}&lt;x_{\text{self}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>nbr</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>self</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}&lt;x_{\text{self}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then this is the optimal representation for Bellman-Ford. However, if <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}&gt;x_{\text{self}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>nbr</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>self</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}&gt;x_{\text{self}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the computation will incorrectly carry forward with <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>nbr</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{self}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>self</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{self}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, by storing a value proportional to <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}-x_{\text{self}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>nbr</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>self</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}-x_{\text{self}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the neuron <span id="A1.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">convs.0.up_mlp.lins.1.6</span> can be rescaled and subtracted from the input to the final update MLP, serving as a correction factor.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The upshot of this calculation is that the update MLP <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Up}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>Up</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Up}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> essentially learns to implement the minimum operation by</p>
<table id="A1.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\{x_{\text{nbr}}^{*},x_{\text{self}}\}=x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}-\ReLU(x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}-x_{\text{self}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>nbr</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>self</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>nbr</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ReLU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>nbr</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>self</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\{x_{\text{nbr}}^{*},x_{\text{self}}\}=x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}-\ReLU(x_{\text{nbr}}^{*}-x_{\text{self}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We also highlight the generalization behavior of the circuit in <a href="#A1.F13" title="In A.3 Bellman-Ford without Self-Loops ‣ Appendix A Additional Results for Bellman-Ford ‣ MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>. While the full model achieves strong generalization performance early, as in <a href="#S5" title="5 Case Study: Bellman-Ford ‣ MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the final circuit does not generalize until much later (around epoch 18000). Examining a circuit extracted from an earlier checkpoint (epoch 10000) in <a href="#A1.F14" title="In A.3 Bellman-Ford without Self-Loops ‣ Appendix A Additional Results for Bellman-Ford ‣ MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, we can see that the connections which are pruned away at this later inflection point (namely, the connections to and from <span id="A1.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">convs.0.up_mlp.lins.0.2</span>) are partially responsible for this same minimum operation in the first update MLP. As in <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Circuit Sparsification ‣ Appendix A Additional Results for Bellman-Ford ‣ MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>, we see <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> regularization encourage the model to align its internal representations with the neuron basis to achieve a sparser solution.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F13" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21442v1/no_self_loop_grokking.svg" id="A1.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/148;" width="476" height="148"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F13.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 13</span>: </span><span id="A1.F13.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Multiplicative test loss, MSE test loss, and <math id="A1.F13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> regularization terms for the full Bellman-Ford network without self-loops and the Bellman-Ford circuit without self-loops. Inset shows a small inflection point in <math id="A1.F13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> norms.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F14" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21442v1/images/bellman-ford-no-self-loop/no_self_loops_circuit_mid_with_colorbar.png" id="A1.F14.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:238/206;" width="238" height="206" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F14.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 14</span>: </span><span id="A1.F14.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Bellman-Ford circuit extracted from epoch 10000 of MinAggGNN training without self-loops. Contains 20 of 18,240 edges.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Results from different runs</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we show relevant plots from different experimental trials.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
